\title{Memory Editing as Algebraic Quotienting}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 20, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Sequential experience may be modeled by the free monoid $A^*$ on an alphabet $A$, while a compositional memory is a monoid homomorphism $M:A^*\to R$ into a representation monoid $R$. If $A$ is nonempty and $R$ is finite, then $M$ necessarily identifies two distinct streams. The relation $u\sim_M v$ defined by $M(u)=M(v)$ is a monoid congruence, the streams mapped to the neutral representation form a submonoid, and the quotient $A^*/{\sim_M}$ is naturally isomorphic to the reachable submonoid $\operatorname{im}(M)$. We then study targeted forgetting, which deletes letters selected by a Boolean retention rule. Every deleted generator lies in the erased submonoid, and every compositional map constant on the fibers of targeted forgetting factors uniquely through its quotient. These results join a finite-state information constraint to the first isomorphism principle for monoids. Algorithms and applications to finite automata, privacy filters, event logging, state abstraction, and sequential feature extraction are discussed.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Many systems receive a sequence of events and preserve only a representation of that sequence. A finite-state controller reads inputs and updates its state. A logging system retains selected event types. A feature extractor converts a token stream into a compact summary. These examples share a compositional ideal: the representation of a concatenated stream agrees with a suitable combination of the representations of its parts.

The natural domain for finite streams is a free monoid. Its multiplication is concatenation, and its identity is the empty stream. If the representation space also carries a monoid structure, a compositional memory is exactly a monoid homomorphism. This viewpoint separates three questions. First, when is information loss inevitable? Second, how is that loss organized? Third, what algebra remains observable after all invisible distinctions have been identified?

If the alphabet has at least one symbol, there are infinitely many finite streams. A finite representation therefore forces collisions. Equal representations define an equivalence relation compatible with concatenation, so memory passes from the stream monoid to a quotient. The quotient by representational equality is naturally isomorphic to the image of the memory map and is therefore exactly the algebra of reachable observations.

A transparent example is targeted forgetting. Given a Boolean predicate on symbols, delete every symbol marked false and retain every symbol marked true. This filter extends from generators to a homomorphism on all words. Its universal property states that every homomorphism insensitive to the distinctions erased by the filter descends uniquely to the corresponding quotient.

\section{Algebraic model}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{monoid} is a set $R$ equipped with an associative operation and an identity $1_R$. Thus $(xy)z=x(yz)$ and $1_Rx=x=x1_R$ for all $x,y,z\in R$.
\end{definition}

Let $A$ be an arbitrary alphabet. The \emph{free monoid} $A^*$ consists of all finite words over $A$, including the empty word $\varepsilon$. Multiplication is concatenation. If $u=a_1\cdots a_m$ and $v=b_1\cdots b_n$, then
\[
uv=a_1\cdots a_mb_1\cdots b_n.
\]
The empty word is the identity. Every function $f:A\to R$ into a monoid extends uniquely to a homomorphism $\widehat f:A^*\to R$ by
\[
\widehat f(a_1\cdots a_n)=f(a_1)\cdots f(a_n),\qquad \widehat f(\varepsilon)=1_R.
\]

\begin{definition}
A \emph{compositional memory} with alphabet $A$ and representation monoid $R$ is a map $M:A^*\to R$ satisfying
\[
M(uv)=M(u)M(v),\qquad M(\varepsilon)=1_R.
\]
Its reachable memory monoid is $\operatorname{im}(M)=\{M(w):w\in A^*\}$.
\end{definition}

Define the erased-stream set
\[
K_M=\{w\in A^*:M(w)=1_R\}
\]
and observational indistinguishability by
\[
u\sim_Mv\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad M(u)=M(v).
\]
The first records streams equivalent to the empty stream; the second records every pair that memory fails to distinguish. In a general monoid, which need not have inverses, the latter contains strictly more structural information.

\section{Finite representations force loss}

\begin{theorem}[Finite-Memory Loss Theorem]
Let $A$ be nonempty, let $R$ be a finite monoid, and let $M:A^*\to R$ be a compositional memory. Then there exist $u,v\in A^*$ such that $u\ne v$ and $M(u)=M(v)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Choose $a\in A$. The words $\varepsilon,a,a^2,a^3,\ldots$ are pairwise distinct because their lengths differ. Thus $A^*$ is infinite. Since $R$ is finite, the map $M:A^*\to R$ cannot be injective.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Nontrivial observational class]
Under the preceding hypotheses, at least one equivalence class of $\sim_M$ contains two distinct streams.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The collision supplied by the theorem is exactly a pair $u\ne v$ satisfying $u\sim_Mv$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Bounded collision witness]
If $|R|=N$ and $a\in A$, then two of $\varepsilon,a,a^2,\ldots,a^N$ have equal memories.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
There are $N+1$ displayed words but only $N$ possible representation states, so the pigeonhole principle applies.
\end{proof}

The collision statement uses only finiteness. Compositionality determines the structure of all collisions and permits substitution of indistinguishable streams inside arbitrary contexts.

\section{Congruences, erasure, and quotients}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{monoid congruence} on a monoid $P$ is an equivalence relation $\sim$ such that $u_1\sim v_1$ and $u_2\sim v_2$ imply $u_1u_2\sim v_1v_2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Kernel congruence]
For every compositional memory $M:A^*\to R$, the relation $\sim_M$ is a monoid congruence.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The equivalence laws follow from equality in $R$. If $M(u_1)=M(v_1)$ and $M(u_2)=M(v_2)$, then
\[
M(u_1u_2)=M(u_1)M(u_2)=M(v_1)M(v_2)=M(v_1v_2).
\]
\end{proof}

In particular, $u\sim_Mv$ implies $xuy\sim_Mxvy$ for every $x,y\in A^*$. A distinction forgotten once remains forgotten in every larger context.

\begin{theorem}[Erased-Stream Submonoid Theorem]
For every compositional memory $M:A^*\to R$, the set $K_M$ is a submonoid of $A^*$. Explicitly, $\varepsilon\in K_M$, and $u,v\in K_M$ imply $uv\in K_M$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The homomorphism identity gives $M(\varepsilon)=1_R$. If $M(u)=M(v)=1_R$, then
\[
M(uv)=M(u)M(v)=1_R1_R=1_R.
\]
\end{proof}

Given a congruence $\sim$ on $P$, the quotient $P/{\sim}$ is the set of equivalence classes, with multiplication $[x][y]=[xy]$. Compatibility makes this independent of representatives.

\begin{theorem}[Observable-Quotient Theorem]
Let $M:A^*\to R$ be a compositional memory. Then
\[
A^*/{\sim_M}\cong\operatorname{im}(M)
\]
as monoids. The natural isomorphism is $\Phi([w])=M(w)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $[u]=[v]$, then $u\sim_Mv$, hence $M(u)=M(v)$, so $\Phi$ is well defined. It preserves products because
\[
\Phi([u][v])=\Phi([uv])=M(uv)=M(u)M(v).
\]
It is onto the image by definition. If $\Phi([u])=\Phi([v])$, then $M(u)=M(v)$, so $[u]=[v]$; hence it is one-to-one.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite Loss-and-Quotient Synthesis]
Let $A$ be nonempty, $R$ finite, and $M:A^*\to R$ compositional. Then: (i) two distinct streams have equal memories; (ii) the completely erased streams form a submonoid; and (iii) the quotient by observational indistinguishability is isomorphic to the reachable memory monoid.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine the Finite-Memory Loss Theorem, the Erased-Stream Submonoid Theorem, and the Observable-Quotient Theorem.
\end{proof}

Finiteness guarantees a nontrivial identification; the isomorphism theorem describes the exact observable object produced by all identifications.

\section{Targeted forgetting}
Let $r:A\to\{0,1\}$ be a retention rule. Define $f_r:A\to A^*$ by
\[
f_r(a)=\begin{cases}a,&r(a)=1,\\ \varepsilon,&r(a)=0.\end{cases}
\]
The unique extension $T_r:A^*\to A^*$ deletes unretained letters while preserving the order of retained letters.

\begin{proposition}[Filtering formula]
For $w=a_1\cdots a_n$,
\[
T_r(w)=f_r(a_1)\cdots f_r(a_n).
\]
Moreover, $T_r(uv)=T_r(u)T_r(v)$ and $T_r(\varepsilon)=\varepsilon$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
This is the defining extension of $f_r$ to the free monoid; splitting the product at the boundary between $u$ and $v$ proves the product law.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Forgotten-Symbol Theorem]
If $r(a)=0$, then the one-letter stream $a$ lies in $K_{T_r}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By definition, $T_r(a)=f_r(a)=\varepsilon$, the identity of $A^*$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Targeted fibers and image]
Two streams $u,v$ satisfy $u\sim_{T_r}v$ if and only if their retained subsequences agree. If $A_r=\{a\in A:r(a)=1\}$, then $\operatorname{im}(T_r)=A_r^*$ and therefore
\[
A^*/{\sim_{T_r}}\cong A_r^*.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The first assertion is the definition of the kernel congruence and the operational description of filtering. Every filtered output uses only letters in $A_r$. Conversely, every word over $A_r$ is fixed by $T_r$, so it is in the image. The quotient statement follows from the Observable-Quotient Theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Universal Property of Targeted Forgetting]
Let $S$ be a monoid and $G:A^*\to S$ a monoid homomorphism. Assume that
\[
T_r(u)=T_r(v)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad G(u)=G(v)
\]
for all $u,v\in A^*$. Then there is a unique homomorphism
\[
\overline G:A^*/{\sim_{T_r}}\to S
\]
such that $\overline G([w])=G(w)$ for every $w$. Equivalently, if $\pi$ is the quotient map, then $G=\overline G\circ\pi$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Define $\overline G([w])=G(w)$. The hypothesis makes this independent of representatives. It is multiplicative since
\[
\overline G([u][v])=G(uv)=G(u)G(v).
\]
Every quotient element has the form $[w]$, so any map with the required factorization is forced to take the value $G(w)$ there. This proves uniqueness.
\end{proof}

Targeted forgetting is also idempotent: $T_r(T_r(w))=T_r(w)$. After one pass only retained letters remain. Thus it is a projection onto $A_r^*$, its fixed points equal its image, and its fibers are precisely its quotient classes.

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Linear targeted filtering}
Scan an input word from left to right and append the current symbol precisely when its retention value is true. For a word of length $n$, the running time is $O(n)$ and the output occupies $O(n)$ space in the worst case. Auxiliary space beyond the output is $O(1)$ for an appendable representation. Filtering the two blocks separately or filtering their concatenation produces the same retained sequence.

\subsection{Repeated-symbol collision search}
Suppose $R$ has $N$ states and fix $a\in A$. Begin at $1_R$, record its occurrence at exponent zero, and successively multiply by $M(a)$. At each exponent $j\le N$, test whether the state was previously seen. If it first occurred at $i<j$, return $(a^i,a^j)$. A collision must occur by exponent $N$. Hash-table lookup gives expected $O(N)$ time and $O(N)$ space; a balanced search tree gives $O(N\log N)$ time.

\subsection{Bounded observational partition}
When $A$ is finite, enumerate all words of length at most $L$, compute their representations, and group equal outputs. The number of words is
\[
1+|A|+\cdots+|A|^L,
\]
which for $|A|>1$ equals $(|A|^{L+1}-1)/(|A|-1)$. Exhaustive inspection is therefore exponential in $L$. It gives a finite window into quotient classes and produces explicit representative collisions.

\subsection{Empirical compositionality check}
For a finite sample $W\subseteq A^*$, compare $M(uv)$ with $M(u)M(v)$ for every $(u,v)\in W^2$. This requires $O(|W|^2)$ comparisons, aside from stream evaluation costs. Such a test can refute compositionality on a sample but cannot prove a universal law on an infinite domain. Targeted filtering satisfies the law by construction.

\section{Examples}

\begin{example}[Parity memory]
Let $A=\{a,b\}$ and $R=\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z$ under addition. Set $M(w)=|w|\bmod 2$. Lengths add under concatenation, so $M$ is compositional. Even words form $K_M$, odd words form the other observational class, and the quotient is isomorphic to $\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Modular counting]
Fix $a\in A$ and $m\ge1$. Define $M(w)=\#_a(w)\bmod m$, where $\#_a(w)$ counts occurrences of $a$. Counts add under concatenation. Two words are indistinguishable exactly when their counts are congruent modulo $m$, and $K_M$ consists of words whose $a$-count is divisible by $m$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Selective event logging]
Take $A=\{\mathrm{login},\mathrm{read},\mathrm{write},\mathrm{logout}\}$ and delete only $\mathrm{read}$. The stream
\[
\mathrm{login}\,\mathrm{read}\,\mathrm{read}\,\mathrm{write}\,\mathrm{logout}
\]
becomes $\mathrm{login}\,\mathrm{write}\,\mathrm{logout}$. Insertions or deletions of read events are invisible. Every downstream homomorphism depending only on the retained event sequence factors uniquely through this quotient.
\end{example}

\section{Applications and limitations}
In finite automata, words induce finitely many recorded state effects, so infinitely many words necessarily coincide observationally. Congruence explains why equivalent words may be substituted inside larger contexts without changing the recorded behavior.

In privacy-oriented preprocessing, selected event categories may be removed before downstream analysis. The universal property supplies an architectural criterion: a downstream compositional analysis insensitive to deleted data factors through the sanitized quotient. This algebraic claim is not a complete security theorem, since side channels and noncompositional dependencies are outside the model.

Logs preserve selected events and omit others. Histories with the same retained log become indistinguishable, and the quotient describes exactly the resolution available to later analysis. Retaining more symbols refines the congruence; deleting more symbols coarsens it.

Sequential features such as modular counts and finite transition summaries compose across blocks. Any finite feature monoid collides on an unbounded stream space. The induced quotient identifies the semantic resolution of the feature.

The framework assumes finite streams, exact equality, and strict compositionality. Real systems can be stochastic, approximate, time-varying, or context-dependent. Those settings call for probabilistic kernels, metrics, or other enriched structures. Finiteness of $R$ is needed only for unavoidable loss; all quotient and factorization results hold for arbitrary monoids. An infinite ambient $R$ with finite image also forces collisions.

\section{Future directions}
Boolean deletion can be generalized to substitutions $A\to B^*$, encompassing redaction, coarsening, and token rewriting. Stochastic memories could replace exact fibers by output distributions and congruences by approximate indistinguishability. Quantitative questions include growth of the largest bounded-length collision class and lower bounds on representation size needed to distinguish prescribed word families.

For a finite-state compositional memory, another problem is to compute the smallest concrete realization of its observable monoid. The quotient theorem identifies the canonical abstract target as the reachable image, while efficient representation and minimization remain algorithmic tasks.

Finally, memories can be ordered by information content. If every distinction forgotten by $M_1$ is forgotten by $M_2$, then the same representative-independence argument gives a factorization of $M_2$ through the quotient induced by $M_1$. This supplies a foundation for compositional abstraction pipelines.

\section{Conclusion}
A compositional memory on finite streams is a monoid homomorphism. A finite representation cannot distinguish all streams over a nonempty alphabet. Equality of representations is a congruence, neutralized streams form a submonoid, and the quotient by indistinguishability is exactly the reachable observable algebra. Targeted deletion is a concrete projection whose deleted generators are erased and whose quotient satisfies a unique factorization property.

The finite-state argument explains why loss must happen; the quotient theorem explains what that loss is. Compositional forgetting is therefore not arbitrary degradation but an organized passage from a free stream space to its observable quotient.

\end{document}

